  The governing asymmetry: [1mbad calling code throws, bad stored data degrades silently.[0m A misspelled preference name is a
  bug I want surfaced immediately; a mangled [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m entry is a condition in the wild that must never break a
  login page.

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                Condition                 │                               Behavior                               │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m with an unregistered name        │ Throw — programmer error                                             │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mset[39m with a value failing its validator   │ Throw — programmer error                                             │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Key absent                               │ All defaults                                                         │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m fails                         │ All defaults; next write overwrites the garbage                      │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Parsed value isn't an object, or [38;5;153mvalues[39m  │ All defaults                                                         │
  │ is missing                               │                                                                      │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ One field fails its validator            │ [1mOnly that field[0m falls back; siblings survive                         │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mversion[39m higher than known                │ All defaults — don't guess at a future shape                         │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access throws (private      │ Fall back to an in-memory store for the page's lifetime; preferences │
  │ mode, storage disabled)                  │  stop persisting, the app keeps working                              │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153msetItem[39m throws (quota)                   │ Caught; in-memory value retained, no crash                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Per-field fallback is what defuses approach A's main weakness — a single bad field no longer costs you every
  preference, only itself.

  Does the model and the failure behavior look right? Then I'll lay out the page integration and the testing/tooling
  question.

